  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure/mid-level ridging will continue to be the primary \ninfluence on our weather through this weekend. High temperatures \nremain nearly steady in the upper 80s and lower 90s, falling to \ninto the 50s and 60s overnight. Moisture associated with Hurricane\nIsaias should still remain east of the local area, though the \nwestern periphery may provide some increase in clouds across W AR \nSaturday. High pressure slides slightly eastward over the weekend,\nallowing for an increase in southerly winds, especially across NE\nOK. This will promote at least limited fire weather conditions, \nespecially along and west of Hwy 75 in NE OK. Mid-upper level \nridging will then reintensify over the Southern Plains Sunday and\nMonday. The net effect will be more of the same, with well above \naverage temperatures, dry conditions, and breezy afternoon winds \npromoting limited fire weather concerns. Meanwhile, low pressure \nwill extend southward along the West Coast and merge with what is \ncurrently Tropical Storm Rachel in the eastern Pacific.\n\nBy Tuesday, this trough will move eastward, with the remnants of \ncurrent tropical storm Simon potentially advecting north-northeast\nwithin the flow. The timing and phasing of each of these systems \nwill be important to determining exact impacts to our area next \nweek. Rain/thunderstorm chances may begin as early as Tuesday for \nour area, with higher potential Wednesday and Thursday as a cold \nfront impinges on the region. Details will become more clear over \nthe coming days, but a pattern change does appear likely next week\nresulting in higher rain chances and cooler temperatures.",
